r' (cursor row/col on screen). On the first read, omit 'cursor'. Use 'screen' to understand the current state of interactive/TUI programs.","name":"terminal_read","parameters":{"properties":{"cursor":{"description":"Optional cursor from a previous terminal_read response. When provided, only output written after that cursor is returned. Omit for the first read or to get the full tail window.","type":"integer"}},"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"Close the terminal session and hide the terminal panel. Kills the running process.","name":"terminal_close","parameters":{"properties":{},"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"Get rust-analyzer compiler diagnostics (errors, warnings) for a Rust file. Always use this after editing .rs files to verify your changes compile. Requires the LSP server to be running (lsp = on in config, or /lsp on).","name":"lsp_diagnostics","parameters":{"properties":{"path":{"description":"The .rs file to check. Default: the most recently edited file, else a workspace-wide summary.","type":"string"},"severity":{"description":"Optional filter: error | warning | hint | info. Default: all severities.","type":"string"}},"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"Jump to where a Rust symbol (function, struct, variable, etc.) is defined. Compiler-accurate via rust-analyzer — far more reliable than grep for finding the real definition. Returns the target file, line, column, and a source excerpt. Use whenever you want to know what a call site or name refers to. Requires the LSP server to be running (lsp = on in config, or /lsp on).","name":"lsp_definition","parameters":{"properties":{"column":{"description":"1-based column number of the symbol (point anywhere on the symbol's name).","type":"integer"},"line":{"description":"1-based line number of the symbol (point anywhere on the symbol's name).","type":"integer"},"path":{"description":"The .rs file containing the symbol reference (absolute path).","type":"string"}},"required":["path","line","column"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"Get the type signature and documentation of any Rust symbol (function, struct, variable, etc.) at a file position, via rust-analyzer. Faster and more accurate than opening and reading the source file. Use whenever you need to understand what a symbol is or does. Requires the LSP server to be running (lsp = on in config, or /lsp on).","name":"lsp_symbol_info","parameters":{"properties":{"column":{"description":"1-based column number of the symbol (point anywhere on the symbol's name).","type":"integer"},"line":{"description":"1-based line number of the symbol (point anywhere on the symbol's name).","type":"integer"},"path":{"description":"The .rs file containing the symbol (absolute path).","type":"string"}},"required":["path","line","column"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"Find every place a Rust symbol is used, via rust-analyzer. Unlike grep, this is compiler-accurate — it won't match same-named symbols in other scopes or files. Returns each reference's file, line, column, and a source excerpt. Prefer this over search_content when working with Rust symbols. Requires the LSP server to be running (lsp = on in config, or /lsp on).","name":"lsp_references","parameters":{"properties":{"column":{"description":"1-based column number of the symbol (point anywhere on the symbol's name).","type":"integer"},"include_declaration":{"description":"Whether to include the declaration site itself in the results. Default: true.","type":"boolean"},"line":{"description":"1-based line number of the symbol (point anywhere on the symbol's name).","type":"integer"},"path":{"description":"The .rs file containing the symbol (absolute path).","type":"string"}},"required":["path","line","column"],"type":"object"}},"type":"function"},{"function":{"description":"List valid code completions (methods, fields, functions, variab