    "content": "Your previous reasoning (interrupted): The user wants me to create an addition to a system prompt for \"Rustama\" (the AI assistant persona - me) with instructions on how to use devtodo for various tasks: adding, removing, marking done, and other useful operations. This way I don't have to read the help every time.\n\nLet me think about what I know from the help output I read earlier:\n\nFrom the help:\n- `-a, --add [ARG]` - Add a note (will prompt for a note if one is not supplied)\n- `-l, --link ARG` - Links another todo file to this one\n- `-g, --graft ARG` - In conjunction with --add or --link, graft the new item to the specified item\n- `-R, --reparent ARG` - Change the parent of the first item index to the second item index (separated by a comma). If no second index is given the item is reparented to the root of the tree. eg. todo -R 1,2\n- `-p, --priority ARG` - In conjunction with --add, set the default priority (default|veryhigh|high|medium|low|verylow)\n- `-e, --edit ARG` - Edit the note that is indexed by the given number\n- `--remove ARG` - Remove the note that is indexed by the given number\n- `-d, --done ARG` - Mark the specified notes (and all children) as done\n- `-c, --comment` - Also manipulate finishing comment for item when viewing or marking as done\n- `-D, --not-done ARG` - Mark the specified notes (and all children) as not done\n- `--global-database ARG` - Specify the database to use if the -G (--global) parameter is used\n- `-G, --global` - Use the database specified by the --global-database option. Defaults to ~/.todo_global\n- `--database ARG` - Change the database from the default (.todo) to the filename specified\n- `-T, --TODO` - Generate a typical TODO output text file from a Todo DB\n- `-A, --all` - Shortcut for the filter '+done,+children' to show all notes\n- `-f, --filter ARG` - Display only those notes that pass the filter. Filter composed of min or max priority level, keywords 'done', 'all' or 'children'. Alternatively regex search filter with '/<expression>'\n- `-v, --verbose` - Display verbosely\n- `--colour ARG` - Override default colours\n- `--mono` - Disable all use of colour\n- `--force-colour` - Force use of colour\n- `--title [ARG]` - Set the title of this directory's todo notes\n- `--date-format ARG` - Format the display of time values\n- `--format ARG` - Specify a display format\n- `--use-format ARG` - Map one format string to another\n\nI should also test a few things to verify behavior before writing instructions, maybe. Let me test some commands to make sure they work as expected:\n\n1. Adding: `devtodo -G -a \"note\"` - let me verify this works with the note as argument\n2. Priority: `devtodo -G -a \"note\" -p high`\n3. Grafting (sub-items): `devtodo -G -a \"note\" -g 1`\n4. Removing: `devtodo -G --remove 2`\n5. Edit: `-e` probably opens an editor - that might be interactive. Hmm, editing might be tricky since it likely opens $EDITOR. I could mention that.\n\nLet me test these commands quickly to verify they work, then clean up after myself. Actually, testing is good practice to verify instructions are accurate. Let me do a few tests:\n\n1. Test adding an item: `devtodo -G -a \"Test item\"` \n2. Test adding with priority: `devtodo -G -a \"Test high priority\" -p veryhigh`\n3. Test grafting to parent: `devtodo -G -a \"Test child item\" -g 2`\n4. List: `devtodo -G`\n5. Remove the test items: `devtodo -G --remove 2` etc.\n\nLet me run these tests to verify, then clean up, then write the system prompt addition.\n\nNote that when I marked done with `-d 1`, exit status 0 and it worked. The `-A` shows all. Note the listing output showed \"- 1.Make sure Rustama can use TODOs\" with dash prefix for done.\n\nAlso `-T` generates TODO text file - maybe writes to TODO file? Probably prints to stdout.\n\nLet me also consider: does `-a` with argument work non-interactively? The help says \"will prompt for a note if one is not supplied\" which implies supplying it as an argument works. Let me verify.\n\nAlso for edit: `-e ARG` edits the note indexed by number — likely opens an editor. In a non-interactive context, that could be a problem, but I can mention `EDITOR` env var. Maybe I can test with EDITOR set to something like `sed -i 's/old/new/'`? That's hacky. Alternatively, devtodo edit might read from stdin? Let me test with a quick approach: `EDITOR='true' devtodo -G -e 2` would do nothing but not hang. Let me test what happens.\n\nLet me run the tests. I'll batch independent commands where possible, but these are sequential/dependent (add then list). Let me do:\n\n1. `devtodo -G -a \"Test item\"` and `devtodo -G -a \"Test high\" -p veryhigh` - these are independent, can batch? They both modify the same DB file — parallel writes might conflict. Better to run sequentially in one bash command with &&.\n\nLet me do it in a few bash calls, sequential.\n\nFirst: add a test item with priority, then list.\n\nActually let me be efficient: run a script in one bash call:\n\n```\ndevtodo -G -a \"Test item alpha\" && devtodo -G -a \"Test item beta\" -p veryhigh && devtodo -G -a \"Test child\" -g 2 && devtodo -G\n```\n\nWait — the graft index: after adding alpha (index 2, since item 1 is the done one), beta would be index 3. Then child grafted to 2. Hmm, indexing — I need to see. Actually in devtodo, done items may not count in default listing. The `-d 1` marked item 1. When I ran `devtodo -G -A -v`, it showed \"1.Make sure Rustama...\" so done item is still index 1. Let me just add items and list to see indices.\n\nThen test remove: `devtodo -G --remove 2` etc. And clean up all test items so the user's list stays clean (only the original done item remains).\n\nAlso test `-T` output? Maybe not needed in the prompt.\n\nAlso test the filter regex: `devtodo -G -f /Test/`.\n\nLet me also check: does devtodo -G alone show done items? Probably not by default (shows only not-done). Let me verify.\n\nLet me run the tests now.\n\nAfter verifying, I'll write