t works. Let me verify.\n\nAlso for edit: `-e ARG` edits the note indexed by number — likely opens an editor. In a non-interactive context, that could be a problem, but I can mention `EDITOR` env var. Maybe I can test with EDITOR set to something like `sed -i 's/old/new/'`? That's hacky. Alternatively, devtodo edit might read from stdin? Let me test with a quick approach: `EDITOR='true' devtodo -G -e 2` would do nothing but not hang. Let me test what happens.\n\nLet me run the tests. I'll batch independent commands where possible, but these are sequential/dependent (add then list). Let me do:\n\n1. `devtodo -G -a \"Test item\"` and `devtodo -G -a \"Test high\" -p veryhigh` - these are independent, can batch? They both modify the same DB file — parallel writes might conflict. Better to run sequentially in one bash command with &&.\n\nLet me do it in a few bash calls, sequential.\n\nFirst: add a test item with priority, then list.\n\nActually let me be efficient: run a script in one bash call:\n\n```\ndevtodo -G -a \"Test item alpha\" && devtodo -G -a \"Test item beta\" -p veryhigh && devtodo -G -a \"Test child\" -g 2 && devtodo -G\n```\n\nWait — the graft index: after adding alpha (index 2, since item 1 is the done one), beta would be index 3. Then child grafted to 2. Hmm, indexing — I need to see. Actually in devtodo, done items may not count in default listing. The `-d 1` marked item 1. When I ran `devtodo -G -A -v`, it showed \"1.Make sure Rustama...\" so done item is still index 1. Let me just add items and list to see indices.\n\nThen test remove: `devtodo -G --remove 2` etc. And clean up all test items so the user's list stays clean (only the original done item remains).\n\nAlso test `-T` output? Maybe not needed in the prompt.\n\nAlso test the filter regex: `devtodo -G -f /Test/`.\n\nLet me also check: does devtodo -G alone show done items? Probably not by default (shows only not-done). Let me verify.\n\nLet me run the tests now.\n\nAfter verifying, I'll write